3 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173703 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173703 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6943d4e1-6ad1-5e69-b921-1f57f743a312" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175845 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175845 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3a/3a62075755fbcaf36e8ca2f424ff4d4cc7b621ad56d7c3d403574f61f0cbea14.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-3b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175845 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "240f4490-9ff4-5e67-af51-aad12174ffef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175845 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ = J_2(-4) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(3)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda=-4,$ and\n\n- a single $1 \\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda=3.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-4) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(3) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ![](images/54/5477cdf9c4f043da7ae388e93a4fd7bb419e0f8558e0236034324b191dcd8d50.png) of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ such that $(A+4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nNow, computing $A+4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -11 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -11 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A+4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -11 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -11 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_2=1-x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n7x_1-11(1-x_3)+10x_3 = 3 \n\\qquad\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\qquad\nx_1= 2-3x_3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/56/566d683278f8728bf61eafe881e4c6ed0a610d24f489fa5794cff16e13437e9a.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=0,$ we get  \n\n![](images/72/7218eea9c3fb866b2dba97b8209fef84f24acaf6a474cb8cacf2bfe4477fafeb.png)\n\nFinally, $a-3b=2-3\\cdot 1 = -1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186175846 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175846 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/58/586f3e99869d2660674227aeec2d6de2b04a66147708595eff53774debd2c083.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that ![](images/8b/8b49621fb335ee584def501332018c8464ff589e8c33f2b2deda4ad7c5d2c408.png) is a Jordan canonical decomposition of ![](images/a3/a37f5ab90218459dc29960667933cef65eff91fcbfcded56da9229f48ec4f083.png) which of the following could be the first column of the matrix ![](images/5d/5dc8315d5be11756de049e790140f53d0494a43c4c125a58cd06602746af41b6.png)\n\n![](images/23/23c974ec978f0c044c2af548dc031b7e0f1e571194c97d83803a4fb77c176612.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c413add7-bd5f-5cee-923c-2a02a9d99655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175846 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix ![](images/dc/dcb9915d0c38c47ed9eb3f72b1b56acbb88f64bc5d798f506e7a07586e809c32.png) may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n![](i