$ such that $(A+3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A+3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A+3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we have ${x}_{1}=1-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}-1,$ respectively. \n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1-{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a+b=2\\cdot 1+(-1)=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186382951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382951 #edb/ref