trix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186399699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1e236ec-23c7-5eb7-a830-7a2578db1027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399699 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-2,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(1) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only \n$z$\nis an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+2I)z=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186399700 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 1